                <div class="c-article-body">
                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs2" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs2-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs2">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs2-content"><p>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is a leading cause of mortality worldwide<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 1" title="Vestbo, J. et al. Global strategy for the diagnosis, management, and prevention of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: GOLD executive summary. Am. J. Respir. Crit. Care Med. 187, 347–365 (2013)." href="/articles/ng.3752#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d16465093e2449">1</a></sup>. We performed a genetic association study in 15,256 cases and 47,936 controls, with replication of select top results (<i>P</i> &lt; 5 × 10<sup>−6</sup>) in 9,498 cases and 9,748 controls. In the combined meta-analysis, we identified 22 loci associated at genome-wide significance, including 13 new associations with COPD. Nine of these 13 loci have been associated with lung function in general population samples<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2" title="Hancock, D.B. et al. Meta-analyses of genome-wide association studies identify multiple loci associated with pulmonary function. Nat. Genet. 42, 45–52 (2010)." href="/articles/ng.3752#ref-CR2" id="ref-link-section-d16465093e2458">2</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 3" title="Repapi, E. et al. Genome-wide association study identifies five loci associated with lung function. Nat. Genet. 42, 36–44 (2010)." href="/articles/ng.3752#ref-CR3" id="ref-link-section-d16465093e2461">3</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 4" title="Soler Artigas, M. et al. Genome-wide association and large-scale follow up identifies 16 new loci influencing lung function. Nat. Genet. 43, 1082–1090 (2011)." href="/articles/ng.3752#ref-CR4" id="ref-link-section-d16465093e2464">4</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 5" title="Hancock, D.B. et al. Genome-wide joint meta-analysis of SNP and SNP-by-smoking interaction identifies novel loci for pulmonary function. PLoS Genet. 8, e1003098 (2012)." href="/articles/ng.3752#ref-CR5" id="ref-link-section-d16465093e2467">5</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="Soler Artigas, M. et al. Sixteen new lung function signals identified through 1000 Genomes Project reference panel imputation. Nat. Commun. 6, 8658 (2015)." href="/articles/ng.3752#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d16465093e2470">6</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 7" title="Wain, L.V. et al. Novel insights into the genetics of smoking behaviour, lung function, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (UK BiLEVE): a genetic association study in UK Biobank. Lancet Respir. Med. 3, 769–781 (2015)." href="/articles/ng.3752#ref-CR7" id="ref-link-section-d16465093e2473">7</a></sup>, while 4 (<i>EEFSEC</i>, <i>DSP</i>, <i>MTCL1</i>, and <i>SFTPD</i>) are new. We noted two loci shared with pulmonary fibrosis<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 8" title="Fingerlin, T.E. et al. Genome-wide association study identifies multiple susceptibility loci for pulmonary fibrosis. Nat. Genet. 45, 613–620 (2013)." href="/articles/ng.3752#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d16465093e2490">8</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 9" title="Fingerlin, T.E. et al. Genome-wide imputation study identifies novel HLA locus for pulmonary fibrosis and potential role for auto-immunity in fibrotic idiopathic interstitial pneumonia. BMC Genet. 17, 74 (2016)." href="/articles/ng.3752#ref-CR9" id="ref-link-section-d16465093e2493">9</a></sup> (<i>FAM13A</i> and <i>DSP</i>) but that had opposite risk alleles for COPD. None of our loci overlapped with genome-wide associations for asthma, although one locus has been implicated in joint susceptibility to asthma and obesity<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 10" title="González, J.R. et al. A common 16p11.2 inversion underlies the joint susceptibility to asthma and obesity. Am. J. Hum. Genet. 94, 361–372 (2014)." href="/articles/ng.3752#ref-CR10" id="ref-link-section-d16465093e2504">10</a></sup>. We also identified genetic correlation between COPD and asthma. Our findings highlight new loci associated with COPD, demonstrate the importance of specific loci associated with lung function to COPD, and identify potential regions of genetic overlap between COPD and other respiratory diseases.</p></div></div></section>
                    
    


                    
                        

                        
